FSTAB BASICS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  /etc/fstab                 Static filesystem table (boot mounts)
  cat /etc/fstab             View current fstab
  sudo nano /etc/fstab       Edit fstab (be careful!)
  sudo vim /etc/fstab        Edit with vim
  findmnt                    Show currently mounted filesystems
  findmnt -t <type>          Filter by filesystem type
  lsblk -f                   List block devices + UUID/LABEL/FSTYPE
  blkid                      Show UUIDs and labels of devices

FSTAB FIELD ORDER
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  <device>  <mountpoint>  <type>  <options>  <dump>  <pass>

  device     UUID=... or /dev/sdX or LABEL=...
  mountpoint /mnt/data  or  /home  etc.
  type       ext4, xfs, btrfs, ntfs, vfat, nfs, cifs ...
  options    defaults, noatime, nofail, x-systemd.automount ...
  dump       0 (almost always)
  pass       0 (no check), 1 (root), 2 (others)

COMMON FSTAB EXAMPLES (LOCAL)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # By UUID (recommended)
  UUID=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults  0  2

  # By LABEL
  LABEL=MyData  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2

  # By device path (less reliable)
  /dev/sdb1  /mnt/backup  xfs  defaults  0  2

  # Swap
  UUID=xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx  none  swap  sw  0  0

  # Read-only
  UUID=...  /mnt/ro  ext4  defaults,ro  0  2

USEFUL MOUNT OPTIONS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  defaults                   rw,suid,dev,exec,auto,nouser,async
  noatime                    Don't update access times (faster)
  nodiratime                 Don't update dir access times
  nofail                     Don't fail boot if mount fails
  x-systemd.automount        Mount on-demand (systemd)
  x-systemd.device-timeout=10s  Timeout for device
  ro                         Read-only
  rw                         Read-write
  user                       Allow non-root to mount
  noauto                     Don't mount at boot (manual only)
  _netdev                    Wait for network (for network shares)

// ┌─[ LINUX :: MANUAL MOUNTING ]─────────────────────────────┐
// │                                                          │
// └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

BASIC MOUNT / UMOUNT
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  mount                      Show all mounted filesystems
  mount -a                   Mount everything in fstab
  mount -a -t <type>         Mount only specific type from fstab
  sudo mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt  Mount device to directory
  sudo mount -t ext4 /dev/sdb1 /mnt
  sudo mount -o ro /dev/sdb1 /mnt   Read-only mount
  sudo umount /mnt           Unmount by mountpoint
  sudo umount /dev/sdb1      Unmount by device
  sudo umount -l /mnt        Lazy unmount (busy filesystem)
  sudo umount -f /mnt        Force unmount (use carefully)

CHECK BEFORE UNMOUNT
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  lsof +f -- /mnt            See what is using the mount
  fuser -m /mnt              Show processes using mount
  fuser -km /mnt             Kill processes using mount

